{"cuisine/cuisine-of-chicago":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-chicago","n":"Cuisine of Chicago","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The culture of Chicago, Illinois is known for the invention or significant advancement of several performing arts, including improvisational comedy, house music, industrial music, blues, hip hop, gospel, jazz and soul."],["The city is known for its Chicago School and Prairie School architecture.  It continues to cultivate a strong tradition of classical music, popular music, dance, and performing arts, rooted in Western civilization, as well as other traditions carried forward by its African-American, Asian-American, European American and  Hispanic and Latino American citizens."],["The city is additionally known for various popular culinary dishes, including deep-dish pizza, the Chicago-style hot dog and the Italian beef sandwich."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-chicago"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Chicago"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Made in Chicago: Stories Behind 30 Great Hometown Bites","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Monica Eng","note":"Origins and recipes of Chicago foods by a Chicago food journalist, published by the University of Illinois Press.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780252087059","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780252087059"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Midwestern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-midwestern-united-states"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/yi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yi-cuisine","n":"Yi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Yi cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/bengali-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bengali-cuisine","n":"Bengali cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Bangladeshi cuisine","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine"],["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Bangladesh","intro":[["Bengali cuisine is the culinary style of Bengal, comprising now most of Bangladesh, Indian states of West Bengal, eastern part of Jharkhand known as Manbhum and a region of Assam known as Barak valley. The cuisine has been shaped by the region's diverse history and climate. It is known for its varied use of flavours including mustard oil, as well as the spread of its confectioneries and desserts. There is a strong emphasis on rice as a staple, with fish traditionally the most common protein. Freshwater fish are preferred to marine fish, although barramundi, known as bhetki, is also common. Meat is also a common protein among Bengalis, with chicken and mutton being the most popular. Beef is popular within the Muslim community. However, meat from any source is consumed far less than fish in Bangladesh. In more recent times, lentils have begun to form a significant part of the diet. Many Bengali food traditions draw from religious and social functions, such as adda, Poila Boishakh, Eid, and Durga Puja."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"bengali-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Chutney","dish/chutney"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Roti","dish/roti"],["Sarbhaja","dish/sarbhaja"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Playful Cooking (Bengali cuisine)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"playfulcooking.com","note":"Bengali section of Kankana Saxena's home cooking blog.","url":"https://www.playfulcooking.com/category/bengali-cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Bong Mom's Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sandeepa Mukherjee Datta","note":"Bengali home cooking and family stories, grown out of the author's long-running blog.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"The Bengali Five Spice Chronicles","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Rinku Bhattacharya","note":"Bengali family recipes adapted for American kitchens, published by Hippocrene.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p477/TheBengaliFiveSpiceChronicles.html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Sodium chloride","ingredient/sodium-chloride"],["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Stock (cooking)","ingredient/stock-cooking"],["Atta","ingredient/atta"],["Atta flour","ingredient/atta-flour"],["Cardamom","ingredient/cardamom"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Kheer (Bengali sweets)","ingredient/kheer-bengali-sweets"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Milk skin","ingredient/milk-skin"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Bangladeshi cuisine","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine"],["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/3/34/Bengali_Fish_Thali.jpg/500px-Bengali_Fish_Thali.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Senjuti Dey","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengali_Fish_Thali.jpg"}},"dish/cabbage-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cabbage-soup","n":"Cabbage soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Cabbage soup may refer to any of the variety of soups based on various cabbages, or on sauerkraut and known under different names in national cuisines. Often it is a vegetable soup, with lentils, peas or beans in place of the meat. It may be prepared with different ingredients. Vegetarian cabbage soup may use mushroom stock. Another variety is using a fish stock. There is also a preference to cook cabbage soup using a pork stock."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cabbage-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cabbage_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Brassica oleracea","ingredient/brassica-oleracea"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Sauerkraut","ingredient/sauerkraut"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Polonist","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polonist.com","note":"Polish recipes and food culture from a Polish-born writer living abroad.","url":"https://www.polonist.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Polish Your Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polishyourkitchen.com","note":"Long-running blog of traditional Polish home recipes.","url":"https://polishyourkitchen.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Olga's Flavor Factory","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"olgasflavorfactory.com","note":"Russian-born home cook Olga's blog, with a dedicated Russian recipes category.","url":"https://www.olgasflavorfactory.com/category/russianrecipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"b